export const PLANS = ["free", "pro", "team"] as const;

export type Plan = (typeof PLANS)[number];

const PRICES: Record<Plan, number> = {
  free: 0,
  pro: 12,
  team: 30,
};

export function priceFor(plan: Plan): number {
  return PRICES[plan];
}

for (const plan of PLANS) {
  console.log(`${plan}: $${priceFor(plan)}/month`);
}

steps.body:
An annotation decides a variable's type. `const routes: Record<string, string> = { ... }` makes `routes` exactly that type, so TypeScript forgets which keys you wrote, and every read gives `string | undefined`.

`satisfies` checks the value against a type but keeps the precise type TypeScript infers:

```typescript
const routes = { home: "/", about: "/about" } satisfies Record<string, string>;
//    ^? const routes: { home: string; about: string; }
console.log(routes.about); // /about
```

A value that is not a string is still an error, but now `routes.about` is a plain `string`, and `routes.blog` is an error because that key does not exist. This mix of checked and precise suits configuration: themes, routes, feature flags, and lookup tables. Like an annotation, `satisfies` runs the excess property check on an object literal, so a misspelled key is caught too.

Use an annotation when a variable should have exactly the declared type, for example one you reassign later. Use `satisfies` when you want both the check and the details.

examples.code:
const routes = {
  home: "/",
  about: "/about",
  contact: "/contact",
} satisfies Record<string, string>;

const about = routes.about;
//    ^? const about: string

console.log(`Home: ${routes.home}`);
console.log(`About: ${about}`);
console.log(`Pages: ${Object.keys(routes).length}`);

examples.explanation:
`satisfies Record<string, string>` checks that every value is a string, but `routes` keeps its own type with three known keys, so `routes.about` is a plain `string`, as the bubble shows. Reading a key that is not there fails before the program runs:

```typescript error
const routes = { home: "/", about: "/about" } satisfies Record<string, string>;
console.log(routes.blog);
```

That error (TS2339) is what you want from a fixed set of routes: a link to a page that does not exist never compiles.

examples.code:
const routes: Record<string, string> = {
  home: "/",
  about: "/about",
};

const blog = routes["blog"];
//    ^? const blog: string | undefined
const about = routes["about"];
//    ^? const about: string | undefined

console.log(blog ?? "no blog page");
console.log(about ?? "no about page");

